# Line endings are part of the deploy contract, not a preference.
#
# This repo is developed on Windows and deployed as Linux containers. Two files in that path are
# read by something that treats a carriage return as data: `docker/backend-entrypoint.sh`, whose
# `#!/bin/sh` line becomes `#!/bin/sh\r` in the kernel's eyes (the container then dies with
# "no such file or directory", naming an interpreter that exists), and any script an operator
# runs from Git Bash, e.g. `bash docker/n8n-import.sh`. backend/Dockerfile strips CR before
# chmod as a belt, but the rescue belongs in the recipe, not only in the image: a checkout with
# core.autocrlf=true would otherwise hand every one of these files to a Linux process with CRLF.
#
# `text eol=lf` keeps the stored blob LF and forces LF on checkout, for every clone, regardless of
# each operator's global autocrlf. The rest is the ordinary normalization the repo already relied
# on (`attr/text=auto` was in effect for tracked files), written down so it is not a coincidence.
* text=auto

# --- executed or parsed by Linux: LF, always ---
*.sh            text eol=lf
*.conf          text eol=lf
Dockerfile      text eol=lf
*Dockerfile     text eol=lf
.dockerignore   text eol=lf
.gitignore      text eol=lf
.env.example    text eol=lf
*.service       text eol=lf
*.json          text eol=lf
*.yml           text eol=lf
*.yaml          text eol=lf
*.py            text eol=lf
*.js            text eol=lf
*.ts            text eol=lf
*.html          text eol=lf
*.css           text eol=lf
*.sql           text eol=lf

# --- Windows-only sources: the tooling that reads them wants CRLF ---
*.iss           text eol=crlf
*.bat           text eol=crlf
*.cmd           text eol=crlf

# --- never touch a binary ---
*.png binary
*.jpg binary
*.jpeg binary
*.gif binary
*.ico binary
*.pdf binary
*.docx binary
*.xlsx binary
*.ttf binary
*.woff binary
*.woff2 binary
*.sqlite3 binary
*.db binary
*.exe binary
